Bast Shoes|Lapti	Light shoes made from bast, the inner bark of a birch tree. They were easy to make but not durable.
Batyushka	Literally “little father,” a respectful form of address for Orthodox ecclesiastics.
Beliye	Porcini, a kind of mushroom.
Bogatyr	A legendary Slavic warrior comparable to a Western European knight-errant.
Boyar	A member of the Kievan or Muscovite aristocracy, ranking below only a knyaz, or prince.
Buran	A snowstorm.
Buyan	A mysterious island in Slavic mythology that can appear and disappear and features in several Russian folktales.
Byzantine Cross|Patriarchal Cross	A cross with a smaller crosspiece above the main crossbar and, sometimes, a slanted crossbar near the foot.
Caffa|Feodosia	A city in Crimea, now called Feodosia. During the era of The Winternight Trilogy, it was controlled by the Genoese.
Cathedral of the Assumption|Cathedral of the Dormition|Uspensky Sobor	A cathedral in the modern Moscow Kremlin commemorating the dormition of the Mother of God and her being taken to Heaven. Its original limestone structure was begun in 1326 and consecrated in 1327; the present building dates to the sixteenth century.
Chudovo	A fictional town on the upper Volga whose name derives from chudo, the Russian word for “miracle.” Several towns in modern Russia share the name.
Chyerti|Chyert|Nyechistiye sili	Devils; collectively, the various spirits of Russian folklore. Nyechistiye sili literally means “unclean forces.”
Dan	Tribute, particularly that owed by the conquered Rus’ to their Mongol overlords.
Devochka	A little girl.
Devushka	A young woman or maiden.
Dochka	Daughter.
Durak|Dura	A fool. Dura is the feminine form.
Dvor	A yard or dooryard: the space between the outbuildings on the property of a highborn medieval Russian.
Ecumenical Patriarch	The supreme head of the Eastern Orthodox Church, based in Constantinople, modern Istanbul.
Ger|Yurt	A portable round tent of felt or skins used by Mongol armies. Most were dismantled nightly, while the finest, used by a khan or host leader, could be transported intact on an ox-drawn platform.
Golden Horde	A Mongol khanate founded by Batu Khan in the twelfth century. It adopted Islam in the early fourteenth century and, at its height, ruled a broad part of Eastern Europe, including Muscovy.
Gospodin	A respectful form of address to a man, more formal than “mister” and sometimes translated as “lord.”
Gosudar	A form of address akin to “Your Majesty” or “Sovereign.”
Grand Prince|Velikiy Knyaz|Grand Duke	The ruler of a major medieval Russian principality such as Moscow, Tver, or Smolensk. Velikiy knyaz is also translated as “Grand Duke.”
Hegumen	The head of an Orthodox monastery, equivalent to an abbot in the Western tradition.
Holy Fool|Yurodivy|Fool in Christ	An ascetic who surrendered worldly possessions. A holy fool’s real or feigned madness was believed divinely inspired, allowing truths to be spoken that others dared not voice.
Iconostasis|Icon-screen	A wall of icons arranged in a prescribed layout that separates the nave from the sanctuary of an Eastern Orthodox church.
Izba	A small wooden peasant house, often decorated with carvings. The plural is izby.
Kasha	Porridge made from grains such as buckwheat, wheat, rye, millet, or barley.
Kokoshnik	A Russian headdress whose style varies by place and period. The term generally denotes the closed headdress of married noblewomen, though maidens also wore open-backed forms or headbands; ordinary women more often wore scarves or kerchiefs.
Kolomna	A town in the Moscow region whose name probably derives from the Old Russian word for a river bend. It served as the historical mustering place for Dmitrii’s army before Kulikovo.
Kremlin	A fortified complex at the center of a Russian city. The word is not limited to Moscow: many historic Russian cities have kremlins, and Moscow itself originally lay entirely within its kremlin.
Kulikovo|Kulikovo Pole|Snipes’ Field	Literally “Snipes’ Field,” the location of the historical Battle of Kulikovo.
Kupavna	A fourteenth-century Russian town about fourteen miles east of Moscow, now part of the greater Moscow metropolitan area.
Kvas	A fermented beverage made from rye bread.
Lesnaya Zemlya|Land of the Forest	Literally “Land of the Forest,” the home village of Vasya, Sasha, and Olga.
Letnik	A light, calf-length woman’s garment with long, wide sleeves, worn over a shift.
Lisichki	A kind of mushroom.
Little Brother|Bratishka	An English rendering of the Russian endearment bratishka, applicable to either an older or younger sibling.
Little Sister|Sestryonka	An English rendering of the Russian endearment sestryonka, applicable to either an older or younger sibling.
Maslenitsa	A feast whose name derives from maslo, “butter.” Originally marking winter’s end, it entered the Orthodox calendar as the celebration before Lent, with round cakes symbolizing the newborn sun and the concluding Day of Forgiveness.
Matyushka	Literally “little mother,” a term of endearment.
Mead	Honey wine produced by fermenting honey and water.
Metropolitan	A high Orthodox Church official. In the Middle Ages, the Metropolitan of the Rus’ was Russia’s highest Orthodox authority and was appointed by the Byzantine Patriarch.
Monastery of the Archangel|Aleksei’s Archangel Michael Monastery|Chudov Monastery	A monastery dedicated to the miracle of the Archangel Michael at Colossae. Founded in 1358 by Metropolitan Aleksei, it was commonly called the Chudov Monastery, from chudo, “miracle.”
Moscow|Moskva	A city founded in the twelfth century by Prince Yury Dolgoruki. Once eclipsed by Vladimir, Tver, Suzdal, and Kiev, it rose to prominence after the Mongol invasion under a succession of Rurikid princes.
Moskva River	The river along which Moscow was founded.
Muscovy|Moscovia|Moscov’	The Grand Duchy or Grand Principality of Moscow. Long a common Western name for Russia, it began as a modest territory north and east of Moscow and expanded greatly from the late fourteenth through early sixteenth centuries.
Muzhik	In English usage, a Russian male peasant; in Russian, the term also suggests a sturdy, simple man of the earth.
Mysh|Mysh’	Mouse.
Neglinnaya River	A river that, with the Moskva, formed a natural moat around early Moscow. It now runs underground within the city.
Ogon|Ogon’	Fire.
Otche Nash	“Our Father,” the opening of the Lord’s Prayer in Old Church Slavonic, the traditional form in which the prayer is memorized and recited.
Outrenya	The Slavonic morning office in an Orthodox monastery, corresponding to matins. It is the last of four night offices and traditionally ends at sunrise.
Oven|Pech’	A large Russian structure used for cooking, baking, and heating. Its flues distribute heat evenly, and families often slept on top of it for warmth.
Patent|Yarlyk	An official decree of the Golden Horde granting a ruler of the Rus’ authority over a territory.
Podsnezhnik	A snowdrop, the small white flower that blooms in early spring.
Posad	A district adjoining but outside a Russian town’s fortified walls, often serving as a trade center and sometimes developing into a town or administrative center.
Povecheriye	The evening office in an Orthodox monastery, corresponding to compline in a Catholic monastery.
Pyos	A dog or cur.
Rubakha	A long-sleeved shift and the principal undergarment of most medieval Russian women.
Rus’	Originally a Scandinavian people who established the Rurikid ruling dynasty in the ninth century at the invitation of Slavic and Finnic tribes. Their name came to denote both the ruled territories and their inhabitants.
Russia	From the thirteenth through fifteenth centuries, no unified polity called Russia existed. The term describes lands sharing culture and language but divided among rival princes who owed allegiance to Mongol overlords.
Russian|Russkiy|Rossiyskiy	Russkiy refers to the Russian people and culture without political boundaries; rossiyskiy refers specifically to the modern Russian state.
Sapogi	Short, round-toed boots assembled from several pieces of leather. In this period, they did not distinguish between the left and right foot.
Sarafan	A shoulder-strapped dress resembling a jumper or pinafore and worn over a long-sleeved blouse. It became common in the early fifteenth century.
Sarai	The capital of the Golden Horde, named from the Persian word for “palace.” Princes of the Rus’ traveled there to pay homage and receive patents from the khan.
Serpukhov	A town about sixty miles south of Moscow, founded during Dmitrii Ivanovich’s reign to guard Moscow’s southern approaches and associated with Prince Vladimir Andreevich.
Starik	An old man.
Synok	An affectionate diminutive of syn, meaning “son.”
Terem	Both the secluded quarters of highborn women in Old Russia and the broader Muscovite practice of such seclusion. These quarters could occupy upper floors, a separate wing, or a connected building.
Tonsure	The ritual cutting of hair as a sign of religious devotion. Eastern Orthodox monasticism recognizes the Rassophore, Stavrophore, and Great Schema as three degrees of dedication.
Trinity Lavra|Trinity Lavra of Saint Sergei	A monastery founded by Saint Sergei Radonezhsky in 1337, about forty miles northeast of Moscow.
Tsar	A title derived from Caesar. In the trilogy’s medieval context it denotes the Byzantine emperor in Constantinople rather than a Russian ruler.
Tsargrad|Constantinople	“City of the tsar,” a name for Constantinople.
Ulus	A nation, tribe, or body of followers.
Vedma|Vyed’ma	A witch or wisewoman.
Verst|Versta	A unit of distance equal to roughly one kilometer, or two-thirds of a mile.
Vladimir	One of the principal cities of medieval Rus’, about 120 miles east of Moscow, traditionally dated to 1108.
Writing Icons	A direct translation of the Russian expression for making icons, which uses the verb “to write.”
Zapona	An ancient outer garment worn by maidens over a long-sleeved shift: a rectangular cloth folded at the shoulders, open at the sides, with a head opening and a belt at the waist.
